## 2. Linear Regression

Given a dataset:

$$
\mathcal D=\{(x_t,y_t)\}_{t=1}^{T}
$$

Assume an unknown linear relationship:

$$
y=W^*x
$$

The objective is to learn an estimate $W$ of $W^*$.

### Prediction and Residual

$$
\hat y_t=Wx_t
$$

$$
r_t=\hat y_t-y_t=Wx_t-y_t
$$

### Matrix Dimensions

| Variable | Dimension | Description |
|---|---|---|
| $x_t$ | $d\times 1$ | Input vector |
| $y_t$ | $d\times 1$ | Target vector |
| $\hat y_t$ | $d\times 1$ | Prediction |
| $W$ | $d\times d$ | Weight matrix |
| $r_t$ | $d\times 1$ | Residual |
| $L(W)$ | Scalar | Loss |
| $\nabla_W L$ | $d\times d$ | Gradient |
| $\Delta W$ | $d\times d$ | Parameter update |

### Loss Function

$$
L(W)=\frac{1}{2T}
\sum_{t=1}^{T}\|Wx_t-y_t\|_2^2
$$

Equivalently,

$$
L(W)=\frac{1}{2T}
\sum_{t=1}^{T}r_t^\top r_t
$$

- $1/T$: averages the loss over training samples.
- $1/2$: cancels the factor of 2 during differentiation.

---

## 3. Gradient Descent

Gradient descent updates model parameters in the negative
gradient direction.

$$
W_{\mathrm{new}}=W-\eta\nabla_W L(W)
$$

Define the parameter update:

$$
\Delta W=-\eta\nabla_W L(W)
$$

Therefore,

$$
W_{\mathrm{new}}=W+\Delta W
$$

where $\eta$ is the learning rate.

### Scalar Gradient Example

Given:

$$
L(w)=\frac12(wx-y)^2
$$

Define:

$$
r=wx-y
$$

By the chain rule,

$$
\frac{dL}{dw}
=
\frac{dL}{dr}\frac{dr}{dw}
$$

Since

$$
\frac{dL}{dr}=r,\qquad
\frac{dr}{dw}=x
$$

we obtain

$$
\boxed{
\frac{dL}{dw}=(wx-y)x
}
$$

The gradient descent update is

$$
w\leftarrow w-\eta(wx-y)x
$$

---

## 4. Inner Product and Outer Product

Let $a,b\in\mathbb R^d$.

### Inner Product

$$
a^\top b=\sum_{i=1}^{d}a_i b_i
$$

### Outer Product

$$
ab^\top=
\begin{bmatrix}
a_1b_1 & \cdots & a_1b_d\\
\vdots & \ddots & \vdots\\
a_db_1 & \cdots & a_db_d
\end{bmatrix}
$$

### Comparison

| Operation | Expression | Output Dimension |
|---|---|---|
| Inner product | $a^\top b$ | $1\times1$ |
| Outer product | $ab^\top$ | $d\times d$ |
| Matrix-vector product | $(ab^\top)c$ | $d\times1$ |
| Vector-scalar product | $a(b^\top c)$ | $d\times1$ |

### Important Identity

By associativity of matrix multiplication,

$$
\boxed{
(ab^\top)c=a(b^\top c)
}
$$

This identity connects linear self-attention with gradient
descent.

---

## 5. Matrix Calculus

For a scalar function $f(W)$, its gradient with respect to
a matrix is

$$
[\nabla_W f]_{ij}
=
\frac{\partial f}{\partial W_{ij}}
$$

Therefore,

$$
\operatorname{shape}(\nabla_W f)
=
\operatorname{shape}(W)
$$

### Common Gradient Formulas

| Function | Gradient |
|---|---|
| $f(x)=a^\top x$ | $\nabla_x f=a$ |
| $f(x)=x^\top x$ | $\nabla_x f=2x$ |
| $f(x)=\frac12\|x\|^2$ | $\nabla_x f=x$ |
| $f(x)=x^\top Ax$ | $\nabla_x f=(A+A^\top)x$ |
| $f(x)=\frac12\|Ax-b\|^2$ | $\nabla_x f=A^\top(Ax-b)$ |
| $f(W)=a^\top Wb$ | $\nabla_W f=ab^\top$ |

### Outer Product as a Matrix Gradient

Given:

$$
f(W)=a^\top Wb
$$

Expanding the matrix multiplication:

$$
f(W)=\sum_i\sum_j a_iW_{ij}b_j
$$

Taking the partial derivative:

$$
\frac{\partial f}{\partial W_{ij}}=a_i b_j
$$

Combining all partial derivatives:

$$
\boxed{\nabla_W f=ab^\top}
$$

Matrix gradients in neural networks often have an outer-product
structure involving the input and the backpropagated error.

### Linear Regression Gradient

The gradient required in Problem 1(a) is

$$
\boxed{
\nabla_W L(W)
=
\frac1T\sum_{t=1}^{T}
(Wx_t-y_t)x_t^\top
}
$$

Therefore,

$$
\boxed{
\Delta W
=
-\frac{\eta}{T}
\sum_{t=1}^{T}
(Wx_t-y_t)x_t^\top
}
$$

Dimension check:

$$
\underbrace{(Wx_t-y_t)}_{d\times1}
\underbrace{x_t^\top}_{1\times d}
\in\mathbb R^{d\times d}
$$

---

## 6. Equivalent Parameter and Target Updates

A parameter update changes the residual to

$$
r_{\mathrm{new}}
=
(W+\Delta W)x-y
$$

Rearranging:

$$
\begin{aligned}
(W+\Delta W)x-y
&=Wx+\Delta Wx-y\\
&=Wx-(y-\Delta Wx)
\end{aligned}
$$

Define

$$
\boxed{\Delta y=-\Delta Wx}
$$

Then

$$
\boxed{
(W+\Delta W)x-y
=
Wx-(y+\Delta y)
}
$$

For the entire dataset:

$$
\begin{aligned}
L(W+\Delta W)
&=
\frac{1}{2T}
\sum_{t=1}^{T}
\|(W+\Delta W)x_t-y_t\|^2\\
&=
\frac{1}{2T}
\sum_{t=1}^{T}
\|Wx_t-(y_t-\Delta Wx_t)\|^2
\end{aligned}
$$

Thus, updating the regression parameters is equivalent,
in terms of residuals and loss, to updating the target
representations while keeping $W$ fixed.

### Comparison

| Property | Parameter Update | Target Update |
|---|---|---|
| Weight | $W+\Delta W$ | $W$ |
| Input | $x$ | $x$ |
| Target | $y$ | $y-\Delta Wx$ |
| Prediction | $(W+\Delta W)x$ | $Wx$ |
| Residual | Identical | Identical |
| Loss | Identical | Identical |

### Numerical Example

Given:

$$
w=1,\quad x=2,\quad y=6,\quad \Delta w=1
$$

| Variable | Original | Parameter Update | Target Update |
|---|---:|---:|---:|
| $w$ | 1 | 2 | 1 |
| $x$ | 2 | 2 | 2 |
| $y$ | 6 | 6 | 4 |
| Prediction | 2 | 4 | 2 |
| Residual | -4 | -2 | -2 |
| Loss | 8 | 2 | 2 |

Important: this equivalence concerns residuals and loss,
not identical predictions or actual parameter training.
The modified target is an internal representation, not a
replacement for the original ground-truth label.

---

## 7. In-Context Learning (ICL)

In-context learning is the ability of a model to adapt to
a task using examples provided in its input context without
updating its model parameters.

### Traditional Learning vs. In-Context Learning

| Property | Traditional Learning | In-Context Learning |
|---|---|---|
| Information source | Training dataset | Context examples |
| Forward pass | Yes | Yes |
| Backpropagation | Yes, for GD training | No |
| Optimizer step | Yes, for GD training | No |
| Parameter update | Yes | No |
| Adaptation | Parameter changes | Context-dependent computation |

### Model Parameters vs. Token Representations

| Property | Model Parameters | Token Representations |
|---|---|---|
| Example | $W_Q,W_K,W_V,W_O$ | $Z,Q,K,V$ |
| Forward pass | Fixed | Computed or updated |
| Optimizer step | Updated | Not directly optimized |
| Dependency | Learned parameters | Current input and parameters |

A forward pass can produce different computations for different
inputs, even though the model parameters remain fixed.

Problem 1(b) investigates a special construction in which a
transformer forward pass simulates one gradient descent step
in linear regression.

This is one theoretical mechanism for ICL, not a universal
explanation of all in-context learning behavior.

---

## 8. Token Representation

Each regression sample is represented as a token:

$$
z_t=
\begin{bmatrix}
x_t\\
y_t
\end{bmatrix}
\in\mathbb R^{2d}
$$

Define the token dimension:

$$
D=2d
$$

The complete context is

$$
Z=[z_1,z_2,\ldots,z_T]
\in\mathbb R^{D\times T}
$$

Each column represents one token.

### Matrix Dimensions

| Variable | Dimension |
|---|---|
| $x_t$ | $d\times1$ |
| $y_t$ | $d\times1$ |
| $z_t$ | $2d\times1$ |
| $Z$ | $2d\times T$ |

The entire regression dataset becomes the transformer's context.

---

## 9. Query, Key, Value (QKV)

Each token is projected into three vectors:

$$
q_t=W_Qz_t
$$

$$
k_t=W_Kz_t
$$

$$
v_t=W_Vz_t
$$

For all tokens:

$$
Q=W_QZ
$$

$$
K=W_KZ
$$

$$
V=W_VZ
$$

| Component | Interpretation |
|---|---|
| Query | Information sought by the current token |
| Key | Features used for matching tokens |
| Value | Information transmitted between tokens |
| Output projection | Transforms the aggregated information |

The attention score between source token $t$ and query token
$j$ is

$$
s_{tj}=k_t^\top q_j
$$

---

## 10. Standard Self-Attention

### Softmax

$$
\operatorname{softmax}(s_i)
=
\frac{\exp(s_i)}
{\sum_j\exp(s_j)}
$$

Properties:

$$
a_i>0,\qquad \sum_i a_i=1
$$

### Scaled Dot-Product Attention

$$
a_{tj}
=
\frac{
\exp(k_t^\top q_j/\sqrt{d_k})
}{
\sum_{s=1}^{T}
\exp(k_s^\top q_j/\sqrt{d_k})
}
$$

The attention output for token $j$ is

$$
\operatorname{Attention}(Z)_j
=
O\sum_{t=1}^{T}a_{tj}v_t
$$

Using the column-token convention:

$$
\operatorname{Attention}(Z)
=
OV\operatorname{softmax}
\left(\frac{K^\top Q}{\sqrt{d_k}}\right)
$$

Softmax is applied over the source tokens for each query.

### Multi-Head Self-Attention

Multiple attention heads operate in different feature spaces.

The unscaled notation used in HW3 is

$$
\operatorname{MHSA}(Z)
=
\sum_{h=1}^{H}
O_hV_h\operatorname{softmax}(K_h^\top Q_h)
$$

---

## 11. Linear Self-Attention (LSA)

Problem 1 considers:

- A single transformer layer.
- A single attention head.
- No MLP.
- No softmax.
- No attention scaling factor.

The attention output is

$$
\boxed{
\operatorname{LSA}_{\theta}(Z)
=
OVK^\top Q
}
$$

where

$$
\theta=\{W_Q,W_K,W_V,W_O\},
\qquad O=W_O
$$

For an individual token:

$$
\boxed{
\operatorname{LSA}_{\theta}(Z)_j
=
O\sum_{t=1}^{T}
v_t(k_t^\top q_j)
}
$$

### Softmax Attention vs. Linear Self-Attention

| Property | Softmax Attention | Linear Self-Attention |
|---|---|---|
| Weight | Normalized exponential | Raw dot product |
| Negative weights | No | Possible |
| Sum of weights | 1 | Unconstrained |
| Softmax | Yes | No |
| Matrix reassociation | Generally not possible | Possible |

Although called linear self-attention, LSA is not linear
with respect to the entire input $Z$, because $Q$, $K$,
and $V$ all depend on $Z$.

### Matrix Dimensions

Using $D=2d$:

| Matrix | Dimension |
|---|---|
| $Z$ | $D\times T$ |
| $W_Q$ | $D\times D$ |
| $W_K$ | $D\times D$ |
| $W_V$ | $D\times D$ |
| $W_O=O$ | $D\times D$ |
| $q_j,k_t,v_t$ | $D\times1$ |
| $Q,K,V$ | $D\times T$ |
| $k_t^\top q_j$ | Scalar |
| $K^\top Q$ | $T\times T$ |
| $v_tk_t^\top$ | $D\times D$ |
| $VK^\top$ | $D\times D$ |
| $OVK^\top Q$ | $D\times T$ |

---

## 12. Alternative Forms of Linear Self-Attention

Starting from

$$
\operatorname{LSA}(Z)_j
=
O\sum_{t=1}^{T}v_t(k_t^\top q_j)
$$

Using associativity:

$$
v_t(k_t^\top q_j)
=
(v_tk_t^\top)q_j
$$

Therefore,

$$
\begin{aligned}
\operatorname{LSA}(Z)_j
&=
O\sum_{t=1}^{T}(v_tk_t^\top)q_j\\
&=
O\left(\sum_{t=1}^{T}v_tk_t^\top\right)q_j
\end{aligned}
$$

Define a context-dependent matrix:

$$
M(Z)=\sum_{t=1}^{T}v_tk_t^\top=VK^\top
$$

Then

$$
\boxed{
\operatorname{LSA}(Z)_j=OM(Z)q_j
}
$$

### Two Equivalent Computation Orders

$$
\boxed{
OV(K^\top Q)=O(VK^\top)Q
}
$$

| Computation | Intermediate Matrix | Dimension |
|---|---|---|
| Attention scores first | $K^\top Q$ | $T\times T$ |
| Context aggregation first | $VK^\top$ | $D\times D$ |

The matrix $M(Z)$ depends on the input context, even though
the transformer parameters remain fixed.

Different contexts can therefore produce different effective
transformations.

---

## 13. Residual Connection

The transformer layer updates the tokens through

$$
\boxed{
Z_{\mathrm{new}}
=
Z+\operatorname{LSA}(Z)
}
$$

For each token:

$$
z_{j,\mathrm{new}}
=
z_j+\operatorname{LSA}(Z)_j
$$

### Comparison with Gradient Descent

| Property | Gradient Descent | Transformer Layer |
|---|---|---|
| Update rule | $W\leftarrow W+\Delta W$ | $Z\leftarrow Z+\operatorname{LSA}(Z)$ |
| Updated object | Model parameters | Token representations |
| Update source | Loss gradient | Attention computation |
| Context dependency | Training samples | Input tokens |

---

## 14. Connecting Linear Self-Attention and Gradient Descent

The regression update is

$$
\Delta W
=
-\frac{\eta}{T}
\sum_{t=1}^{T}
(Wx_t-y_t)x_t^\top
$$

The equivalent target update is

$$
\Delta y_j=-\Delta Wx_j
$$

Substituting the regression update:

$$
\Delta y_j
=
\frac{\eta}{T}
\sum_{t=1}^{T}
(Wx_t-y_t)x_t^\top x_j
$$

By associativity:

$$
\boxed{
\Delta y_j
=
\frac{\eta}{T}
\sum_{t=1}^{T}
(Wx_t-y_t)(x_t^\top x_j)
}
$$

Compare this with

$$
\operatorname{LSA}(Z)_j
=
O\sum_{t=1}^{T}v_t(k_t^\top q_j)
$$

### Structural Correspondence

| Gradient Descent | Linear Self-Attention |
|---|---|
| Training sample $(x_t,y_t)$ | Token $z_t$ |
| Residual $Wx_t-y_t$ | Value information |
| Training input $x_t$ | Key information |
| Current input $x_j$ | Query information |
| $x_t^\top x_j$ | Key-query inner product |
| Sum over training samples | Sum over context tokens |
| $\eta/T$ | Scaling through projection matrices |
| Equivalent target update | Attention output |

The construction required in Problem 1(b) must satisfy

$$
\boxed{
\operatorname{LSA}_{\theta}(Z)_j
=
\begin{bmatrix}
0\\
-\Delta Wx_j
\end{bmatrix}
}
$$

Consequently, the residual connection gives

$$
\begin{aligned}
z_{j,\mathrm{new}}
&=
\begin{bmatrix}
x_j\\
y_j
\end{bmatrix}
+
\begin{bmatrix}
0\\
-\Delta Wx_j
\end{bmatrix}\\
&=
\begin{bmatrix}
x_j\\
y_j-\Delta Wx_j
\end{bmatrix}
\end{aligned}
$$

Requirements:

- The input component $x_j$ remains unchanged.
- The target representation $y_j$ is updated.
- The regression matrix $W$ remains unchanged.
- The transformer parameters $\theta$ remain unchanged.
- The attention update depends on the entire dataset.
- $\theta$ may depend on $W$, $\eta$, and $T$.
- $\theta$ must not depend on the specific dataset.

There are two distinct sets of parameters:

| Parameters | Role |
|---|---|
| $W$ | Regression model being simulated |
| $\theta=\{W_Q,W_K,W_V,W_O\}$ | Transformer performing the simulation |

The key idea is that attention performs a context-dependent
computation equivalent to a regression gradient update in
terms of the resulting residuals.

---

## 15. Block Matrices

Given

$$
z=
\begin{bmatrix}
x\\y
\end{bmatrix}
$$

and

$$
A=
\begin{bmatrix}
A_{11}&A_{12}\\
A_{21}&A_{22}
\end{bmatrix}
$$

where each block is $d\times d$, we have

$$
Az=
\begin{bmatrix}
A_{11}x+A_{12}y\\
A_{21}x+A_{22}y
\end{bmatrix}
$$

### Common Block Matrix Operations

| Operation | Block Matrix | Output |
|---|---|---|
| Keep upper component | $\begin{bmatrix}I&0\\0&0\end{bmatrix}$ | $\begin{bmatrix}x\\0\end{bmatrix}$ |
| Keep lower component | $\begin{bmatrix}0&0\\0&I\end{bmatrix}$ | $\begin{bmatrix}0\\y\end{bmatrix}$ |
| Swap components | $\begin{bmatrix}0&I\\I&0\end{bmatrix}$ | $\begin{bmatrix}y\\x\end{bmatrix}$ |
| Difference | $\begin{bmatrix}0&0\\I&-I\end{bmatrix}$ | $\begin{bmatrix}0\\x-y\end{bmatrix}$ |

Block matrices allow us to construct projections that extract,
combine, or transform specific components of a token.

---

## 16. Essential Formula Summary

| Concept | Formula |
|---|---|
| Linear regression | $\hat y=Wx$ |
| Residual | $r=Wx-y$ |
| Regression loss | $L(W)=\frac1{2T}\sum_t\|Wx_t-y_t\|^2$ |
| Matrix gradient | $\nabla_W L=\frac1T\sum_t(Wx_t-y_t)x_t^\top$ |
| Gradient descent | $\Delta W=-\eta\nabla_W L$ |
| Equivalent target update | $\Delta y=-\Delta Wx$ |
| Outer-product identity | $(ab^\top)c=a(b^\top c)$ |
| Token representation | $z_t=[x_t^\top,y_t^\top]^\top$ |
| Query projection | $Q=W_QZ$ |
| Key projection | $K=W_KZ$ |
| Value projection | $V=W_VZ$ |
| Linear self-attention | $\operatorname{LSA}(Z)=OVK^\top Q$ |
| Single-token attention | $\operatorname{LSA}(Z)_j=O\sum_t v_t(k_t^\top q_j)$ |
| Context aggregation | $M(Z)=VK^\top$ |
| Residual connection | $Z_{\mathrm{new}}=Z+\operatorname{LSA}(Z)$ |

---

## 17. Common Mistakes

- Confusing the regression matrix $W$ with the transformer
  parameters $\theta$.
- Confusing inner products with outer products.
- Forgetting that matrix gradients have the same shape as
  the parameters.
- Applying softmax in the linear self-attention construction.
- Forgetting the negative sign in $\Delta y=-\Delta Wx$.
- Assuming equivalent residuals imply identical predictions.
- Assuming a forward pass updates model parameters.
- Assuming all in-context learning is implemented through
  gradient descent.
- Assuming linear self-attention is linear in the entire
  input matrix $Z$.

---

## 18. Key Takeaways

1. Linear regression gradients have an outer-product structure.
2. Linear self-attention also aggregates outer products.
3. A regression parameter update can be expressed as an
   equivalent update to target representations.
4. Transformer parameters can remain fixed while attention
   computes dataset-dependent transformations.
5. Under a suitable construction, a transformer forward pass
   can simulate one gradient descent step in linear regression
   through an equivalent token update.


   # HW3 Problem 2 — Diffusion with a Mixture of Gaussians

# Part I — Mathematical Foundations

## 1. Expectation and Covariance

### 1.1 Expectation

For a continuous random vector:

$$
\mu_x=\mathbb E[x]=\int xp(x)\,dx
$$

Linearity of expectation:

$$
\mathbb E[Ax+b]=A\mathbb E[x]+b
$$

$$
\mathbb E[x+y]=\mathbb E[x]+\mathbb E[y]
$$

Linearity does not require independence.

### 1.2 Covariance

$$
\boxed{
\operatorname{Cov}(x)
=
\mathbb E[(x-\mu_x)(x-\mu_x)^\top]
}
$$

Equivalently,

$$
\operatorname{Cov}(x)
=
\mathbb E[xx^\top]-\mu_x\mu_x^\top
$$

For zero-mean random variables:

$$
\boxed{
\operatorname{Cov}(x)=\mathbb E[xx^\top]
}
$$

For a two-dimensional random vector:

$$
\Sigma=
\begin{bmatrix}
\operatorname{Var}(x_1)&\operatorname{Cov}(x_1,x_2)\\
\operatorname{Cov}(x_2,x_1)&\operatorname{Var}(x_2)
\end{bmatrix}
$$

- Diagonal entries: individual variances.
- Off-diagonal entries: covariance between dimensions.

### 1.3 Cross-Covariance

$$
\boxed{
\operatorname{Cov}(x,y)
=
\mathbb E[(x-\mu_x)(y-\mu_y)^\top]
}
$$

For zero-mean random variables:

$$
\operatorname{Cov}(x,y)=\mathbb E[xy^\top]
$$

Important properties:

$$
\operatorname{Cov}(x,y)
=
\operatorname{Cov}(y,x)^\top
$$

$$
\operatorname{Cov}(Ax,By)
=
A\operatorname{Cov}(x,y)B^\top
$$

$$
x\perp y
\implies
\operatorname{Cov}(x,y)=0
$$

Zero covariance does not generally imply independence.
However, it does imply independence for jointly Gaussian
random variables.

### 1.4 Covariance of a Sum

$$
\begin{aligned}
\operatorname{Cov}(x+y)
={}&\operatorname{Cov}(x)
+\operatorname{Cov}(y)\\
&+\operatorname{Cov}(x,y)
+\operatorname{Cov}(y,x)
\end{aligned}
$$

If $x$ and $y$ are independent:

$$
\boxed{
\operatorname{Cov}(x+y)
=
\operatorname{Cov}(x)+\operatorname{Cov}(y)
}
$$

### 1.5 Matrix Dimensions

Let $x\in\mathbb R^d$ and $y\in\mathbb R^m$.

| Variable | Dimension |
|---|---|
| $x$ | $d\times1$ |
| $y$ | $m\times1$ |
| $\mathbb E[x]$ | $d\times1$ |
| $\mathbb E[y]$ | $m\times1$ |
| $\operatorname{Cov}(x)$ | $d\times d$ |
| $\operatorname{Cov}(y)$ | $m\times m$ |
| $\operatorname{Cov}(x,y)$ | $d\times m$ |
| $\operatorname{Cov}(y,x)$ | $m\times d$ |

---

## 2. Matrix Foundations

### 2.1 Positive Definite Matrix

A symmetric matrix $A$ is positive definite if

$$
v^\top Av>0,\qquad \forall v\neq0
$$

Notation:

$$
A\succ0
$$

Properties:

- All eigenvalues are positive.
- $A$ is invertible.
- $\det(A)>0$.
- $A^{-1}$ is symmetric positive definite.

Covariance matrices are positive semidefinite in general.
A nondegenerate Gaussian distribution has a positive definite
covariance matrix.

### 2.2 Matrix Transpose and Inverse

$$
(AB)^\top=B^\top A^\top
$$

$$
(AB)^{-1}=B^{-1}A^{-1}
$$

$$
(A^{-1})^\top=(A^\top)^{-1}
$$

If $A$ is symmetric and invertible:

$$
(A^{-1})^\top=A^{-1}
$$

### 2.3 Matrix Commutativity

In general,

$$
AB\neq BA
$$

However, if

$$
B=aA+bI
$$

then

$$
AB=A(aA+bI)=aA^2+bA
$$

$$
BA=(aA+bI)A=aA^2+bA
$$

Therefore,

$$
AB=BA
$$

If $B$ is invertible:

$$
AB^{-1}=B^{-1}A
$$

### 2.4 Useful Matrix Identity

Given

$$
B=aA+bI
$$

we have

$$
\begin{aligned}
I-aAB^{-1}
&=BB^{-1}-aAB^{-1}\\
&=(B-aA)B^{-1}\\
&=bB^{-1}
\end{aligned}
$$

Therefore,

$$
\boxed{
I-aAB^{-1}=bB^{-1}
}
$$

Similarly,

$$
\boxed{
I-bB^{-1}=aAB^{-1}
}
$$

These identities are useful for simplifying diffusion
covariance and denoising expressions.

### 2.5 Quadratic Form and Matrix Gradient

A quadratic form is defined as

$$
f(x)=x^\top Ax
$$

Its gradient is

$$
\boxed{
\nabla_x(x^\top Ax)=(A+A^\top)x
}
$$

If $A$ is symmetric:

$$
\boxed{
\nabla_x(x^\top Ax)=2Ax
}
$$

For a shifted quadratic form:

$$
\nabla_x[(x-\mu)^\top A(x-\mu)]
=
(A+A^\top)(x-\mu)
$$

### Gradient Formula Table

| Function | Gradient w.r.t. $x$ |
|---|---|
| $a^\top x$ | $a$ |
| $x^\top x$ | $2x$ |
| $\frac12x^\top x$ | $x$ |
| $x^\top Ax$ | $(A+A^\top)x$ |
| $x^\top Ax$, symmetric $A$ | $2Ax$ |
| $\frac12(x-\mu)^\top A(x-\mu)$, symmetric $A$ | $A(x-\mu)$ |
| $-\frac12(x-\mu)^\top A(x-\mu)$, symmetric $A$ | $-A(x-\mu)$ |

### 2.6 Schur Complement

Given a block matrix

$$
M=
\begin{bmatrix}
A&B\\
C&D
\end{bmatrix}
$$

where $D$ is invertible, the Schur complement of $D$ is

$$
\boxed{
S=A-BD^{-1}C
}
$$

This matrix structure appears in the conditional covariance
of jointly Gaussian random variables.

