## 3. Multivariate Gaussian Distribution

### 3.1 Definition

$$
x\sim\mathcal N(\mu,\Sigma)
$$

The probability density function is

$$
\boxed{
p(x)=
\frac{1}{(2\pi)^{d/2}|\Sigma|^{1/2}}
\exp\left[
-\frac12(x-\mu)^\top
\Sigma^{-1}(x-\mu)
\right]
}
$$

### Matrix Dimensions

| Variable | Dimension | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| $x$ | $d\times1$ | Random vector |
| $\mu$ | $d\times1$ | Mean vector |
| $\Sigma$ | $d\times d$ | Covariance matrix |
| $\Sigma^{-1}$ | $d\times d$ | Precision matrix |
| $\det(\Sigma)$ | Scalar | Determinant |
| $p(x)$ | Scalar | Probability density |
| $(x-\mu)^\top\Sigma^{-1}(x-\mu)$ | Scalar | Squared Mahalanobis distance |

### 3.2 Special Gaussian Distributions

| Distribution | Meaning |
|---|---|
| $\mathcal N(0,\Sigma)$ | Zero-mean Gaussian |
| $\mathcal N(\mu,\sigma^2I)$ | Isotropic Gaussian |
| $\mathcal N(0,I)$ | Standard multivariate Gaussian |
| $\mathcal N(\mu,\operatorname{diag}(\sigma_1^2,\ldots,\sigma_d^2))$ | Diagonal covariance Gaussian |

### 3.3 Precision Matrix

$$
\boxed{
\Lambda=\Sigma^{-1}
}
$$

A large precision along a direction corresponds to a small
variance along that direction.

