## 4. Gaussian Affine Transformation

### 4.1 General Formula

Given

$$
x\sim\mathcal N(\mu,\Sigma)
$$

and

$$
y=Ax+b
$$

then

$$
\boxed{
y\sim\mathcal N(A\mu+b,A\Sigma A^\top)
}
$$

The transformed Gaussian may be degenerate if $A$ does not
have sufficient rank.

### 4.2 Derivation of Mean

$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathbb E[y]
&=\mathbb E[Ax+b]\\
&=A\mathbb E[x]+b\\
&=A\mu+b
\end{aligned}
$$

### 4.3 Derivation of Covariance

$$
y-\mu_y=A(x-\mu)
$$

Therefore,

$$
\begin{aligned}
\operatorname{Cov}(y)
&=\mathbb E[(y-\mu_y)(y-\mu_y)^\top]\\
&=\mathbb E[A(x-\mu)(x-\mu)^\top A^\top]\\
&=A\Sigma A^\top
\end{aligned}
$$

### 4.4 Scalar Scaling

$$
\boxed{
\operatorname{Cov}(ax)=a^2\operatorname{Cov}(x)
}
$$

Example:

$$
x\sim\mathcal N(0,4)
$$

$$
2x\sim\mathcal N(0,16)
$$

Variance scales with the square of the coefficient.

### 4.5 Sum of Independent Gaussian Variables

Given independent Gaussian variables:

$$
x\sim\mathcal N(\mu_x,\Sigma_x)
$$

$$
z\sim\mathcal N(\mu_z,\Sigma_z)
$$

then

$$
\boxed{
x+z\sim
\mathcal N(\mu_x+\mu_z,\Sigma_x+\Sigma_z)
}
$$

### 4.6 Linear Transformation with Gaussian Noise

Given

$$
x\sim\mathcal N(\mu,\Sigma)
$$

$$
\epsilon\sim\mathcal N(0,I),\qquad x\perp\epsilon
$$

and

$$
y=Ax+B\epsilon+b
$$

then

$$
\boxed{
y\sim
\mathcal N(A\mu+b,A\Sigma A^\top+BB^\top)
}
$$

### Matrix Dimensions

| Variable | Dimension |
|---|---|
| $x$ | $d\times1$ |
| $\Sigma$ | $d\times d$ |
| $A$ | $m\times d$ |
| $\epsilon$ | $r\times1$ |
| $B$ | $m\times r$ |
| $b$ | $m\times1$ |
| $y$ | $m\times1$ |
| $A\Sigma A^\top$ | $m\times m$ |
| $BB^\top$ | $m\times m$ |

