## 5. Joint, Conditional and Marginal Distributions

### 5.1 Joint Distribution

$$
p(x,y)
$$

Describes the distribution and dependence of two random
variables.

### 5.2 Conditional Distribution

$$
\boxed{
p(x\mid y)=\frac{p(x,y)}{p(y)}
}
$$

### 5.3 Marginal Distribution

For continuous variables:

$$
\boxed{
p(x)=\int p(x,y)\,dy
}
$$

For discrete variables:

$$
\boxed{
p(x)=\sum_k p(x,c=k)
}
$$

### 5.4 Bayes' Rule

$$
\boxed{
p(x\mid y)
=
\frac{p(y\mid x)p(x)}{p(y)}
}
$$

$$
\text{Posterior}
=
\frac{\text{Likelihood}\times\text{Prior}}
{\text{Evidence}}
$$

| Term | Expression |
|---|---|
| Prior | $p(x)$ |
| Likelihood | $p(y\mid x)$ |
| Evidence | $p(y)$ |
| Posterior | $p(x\mid y)$ |

### 5.5 Law of Total Probability

$$
p(x)=\sum_k p(x\mid c=k)p(c=k)
$$

Conditional version:

$$
\boxed{
p(x\mid y)
=
\sum_k p(x\mid y,c=k)p(c=k\mid y)
}
$$

### 5.6 Law of Total Expectation

$$
\mathbb E[x]
=
\mathbb E_y[\mathbb E[x\mid y]]
$$

Conditional version for a discrete latent variable:

$$
\boxed{
\mathbb E[x\mid y]
=
\sum_k p(c=k\mid y)
\mathbb E[x\mid y,c=k]
}
$$

---

## 6. Gaussian Mixture Model (GMM)

### 6.1 Definition

$$
\boxed{
p(x)=
\sum_{k=1}^{K}
\pi_k\mathcal N(x;\mu_k,\Sigma_k)
}
$$

Constraints:

$$
\pi_k\geq0,\qquad
\sum_{k=1}^{K}\pi_k=1
$$

### 6.2 Latent Component Variable

Introduce a categorical latent variable:

$$
c\sim\operatorname{Categorical}(\pi_1,\ldots,\pi_K)
$$

$$
p(c=k)=\pi_k
$$

$$
x\mid c=k\sim\mathcal N(\mu_k,\Sigma_k)
$$

Marginalizing the component variable:

$$
p(x)
=
\sum_k p(x\mid c=k)p(c=k)
$$

### 6.3 Posterior Responsibility

Using Bayes' rule:

$$
\boxed{
w_k(x)
=
p(c=k\mid x)
=
\frac{
\pi_k\mathcal N(x;\mu_k,\Sigma_k)
}{
\sum_j\pi_j\mathcal N(x;\mu_j,\Sigma_j)
}
}
$$

Properties:

$$
w_k(x)\geq0
$$

$$
\sum_k w_k(x)=1
$$

### Prior vs. Posterior Weights

| Property | Prior Weight | Posterior Responsibility |
|---|---|---|
| Notation | $\pi_k$ | $w_k(x)$ |
| Definition | $p(c=k)$ | $p(c=k\mid x)$ |
| Input-dependent | No | Yes |
| Sum | 1 | 1 |
| Meaning | Overall component probability | Component probability given an observation |

### 6.4 Conditional Gaussian Mixture

Given a joint Gaussian mixture,

$$
p(x,y)=\sum_k\pi_kp_k(x,y)
$$

conditioning on $y$ gives

$$
\boxed{
p(x\mid y)
=
\sum_k
w_k(y)p(x\mid y,c=k)
}
$$

Since each component is jointly Gaussian,

$$
p(x\mid y)
=
\sum_k
w_k(y)
\mathcal N(x;\mu_{k\mid y},\Sigma_{k\mid y})
$$

A conditional Gaussian mixture remains a Gaussian mixture.

Its weights and component means depend on the observation.
Individual Gaussian conditional covariances are independent
of the observed value.

---

## 7. Joint Gaussian Distribution

### 7.1 General Form

$$
\boxed{
\begin{bmatrix}
x\\y
\end{bmatrix}
\sim
\mathcal N
\left(
\begin{bmatrix}
\mu_x\\\mu_y
\end{bmatrix},
\begin{bmatrix}
\Sigma_{xx}&\Sigma_{xy}\\
\Sigma_{yx}&\Sigma_{yy}
\end{bmatrix}
\right)
}
$$

where

$$
\Sigma_{xx}=\operatorname{Cov}(x)
$$

$$
\Sigma_{yy}=\operatorname{Cov}(y)
$$

$$
\Sigma_{xy}=\operatorname{Cov}(x,y)
$$

$$
\Sigma_{yx}=\Sigma_{xy}^\top
$$

### Matrix Dimensions

| Variable | Dimension |
|---|---|
| $x$ | $d\times1$ |
| $y$ | $m\times1$ |
| Joint vector | $(d+m)\times1$ |
| Joint mean | $(d+m)\times1$ |
| $\Sigma_{xx}$ | $d\times d$ |
| $\Sigma_{xy}$ | $d\times m$ |
| $\Sigma_{yx}$ | $m\times d$ |
| $\Sigma_{yy}$ | $m\times m$ |
| Joint covariance | $(d+m)\times(d+m)$ |

### 7.2 Gaussian Marginalization

Given the joint distribution above:

$$
x\sim\mathcal N(\mu_x,\Sigma_{xx})
$$

$$
y\sim\mathcal N(\mu_y,\Sigma_{yy})
$$

Gaussian marginal distributions are obtained by selecting
the corresponding mean vectors and covariance blocks.

Individual Gaussian marginals do not necessarily imply
a jointly Gaussian distribution.

### 7.3 Constructing a Joint Gaussian

Given

$$
x\sim\mathcal N(\mu_x,\Sigma_x)
$$

$$
\epsilon\sim\mathcal N(0,R)
$$

$$
x\perp\epsilon
$$

and

$$
y=Ax+b+\epsilon
$$

we can write

$$
\begin{bmatrix}
x\\y
\end{bmatrix}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
I&0\\
A&I
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
x\\\epsilon
\end{bmatrix}
+
\begin{bmatrix}
0\\b
\end{bmatrix}
$$

Since independent Gaussian variables are jointly Gaussian,
the affine transformation is also jointly Gaussian.

The mean and covariance of $y$ are

$$
\mu_y=A\mu_x+b
$$

$$
\Sigma_{yy}=A\Sigma_xA^\top+R
$$

The cross-covariance is

$$
\begin{aligned}
\operatorname{Cov}(x,y)
&=\operatorname{Cov}(x,Ax+b+\epsilon)\\
&=\Sigma_xA^\top
\end{aligned}
$$

Therefore,

$$
\boxed{
\begin{bmatrix}
x\\y
\end{bmatrix}
\sim
\mathcal N
\left(
\begin{bmatrix}
\mu_x\\A\mu_x+b
\end{bmatrix},
\begin{bmatrix}
\Sigma_x&\Sigma_xA^\top\\
A\Sigma_x&A\Sigma_xA^\top+R
\end{bmatrix}
\right)
}
$$

