## 8. Gaussian Conditioning

### 8.1 Conditional Gaussian Formula

Given jointly Gaussian random variables:

$$
\begin{bmatrix}
x\\y
\end{bmatrix}
\sim
\mathcal N
\left(
\begin{bmatrix}
\mu_x\\\mu_y
\end{bmatrix},
\begin{bmatrix}
\Sigma_{xx}&\Sigma_{xy}\\
\Sigma_{yx}&\Sigma_{yy}
\end{bmatrix}
\right)
$$

with invertible $\Sigma_{yy}$:

$$
x\mid y\sim
\mathcal N(\mu_{x\mid y},\Sigma_{x\mid y})
$$

Conditional mean:

$$
\boxed{
\mu_{x\mid y}
=
\mu_x+
\Sigma_{xy}\Sigma_{yy}^{-1}(y-\mu_y)
}
$$

Conditional covariance:

$$
\boxed{
\Sigma_{x\mid y}
=
\Sigma_{xx}
-
\Sigma_{xy}\Sigma_{yy}^{-1}\Sigma_{yx}
}
$$

### 8.2 Interpretation

Define the gain matrix:

$$
G=\Sigma_{xy}\Sigma_{yy}^{-1}
$$

Then

$$
\mu_{x\mid y}=\mu_x+G(y-\mu_y)
$$

$$
\Sigma_{x\mid y}=\Sigma_{xx}-G\Sigma_{yx}
$$

| Quantity | Interpretation |
|---|---|
| $\mu_x$ | Prior estimate |
| $y-\mu_y$ | Innovation |
| $G$ | Correction gain |
| $\mu_{x\mid y}$ | Posterior estimate |
| $\Sigma_{xx}$ | Prior uncertainty |
| $\Sigma_{x\mid y}$ | Posterior uncertainty |

For jointly Gaussian variables:

$$
\Sigma_{x\mid y}\preceq\Sigma_{xx}
$$

The conditional mean depends on the observation $y$.
The conditional covariance does not depend on its value.

### 8.3 Derivation of Gaussian Conditioning

Define

$$
G=\Sigma_{xy}\Sigma_{yy}^{-1}
$$

and

$$
u=x-\mu_x-G(y-\mu_y)
$$

Since $u$ is an affine transformation of jointly Gaussian
variables, $u$ and $y$ are jointly Gaussian.

Mean:

$$
\mathbb E[u]=0
$$

Cross-covariance:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\operatorname{Cov}(u,y)
&=\Sigma_{xy}-G\Sigma_{yy}\\
&=\Sigma_{xy}
-\Sigma_{xy}\Sigma_{yy}^{-1}\Sigma_{yy}\\
&=0
\end{aligned}
$$

Joint Gaussianity and zero cross-covariance imply

$$
u\perp y
$$

Since

$$
x=\mu_x+G(y-\mu_y)+u
$$

the conditional mean is

$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathbb E[x\mid y]
&=\mu_x+G(y-\mu_y)+\mathbb E[u\mid y]\\
&=\mu_x+G(y-\mu_y)
\end{aligned}
$$

The conditional covariance is

$$
\operatorname{Cov}(x\mid y)
=
\operatorname{Cov}(u)
$$

Expanding:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\operatorname{Cov}(u)
={}&\Sigma_{xx}
-G\Sigma_{yx}
-\Sigma_{xy}G^\top\\
&+G\Sigma_{yy}G^\top
\end{aligned}
$$

Using

$$
G\Sigma_{yy}=\Sigma_{xy}
$$

gives

$$
\operatorname{Cov}(u)
=
\Sigma_{xx}-G\Sigma_{yx}
$$

Therefore,

$$
\boxed{
\operatorname{Cov}(x\mid y)
=
\Sigma_{xx}
-\Sigma_{xy}\Sigma_{yy}^{-1}\Sigma_{yx}
}
$$

### 8.4 Matrix Dimensions

| Expression | Dimension |
|---|---|
| $\Sigma_{xy}$ | $d\times m$ |
| $\Sigma_{yy}^{-1}$ | $m\times m$ |
| $\Sigma_{yx}$ | $m\times d$ |
| $G$ | $d\times m$ |
| $y-\mu_y$ | $m\times1$ |
| $\mu_{x\mid y}$ | $d\times1$ |
| $\Sigma_{xy}\Sigma_{yy}^{-1}\Sigma_{yx}$ | $d\times d$ |
| $\Sigma_{x\mid y}$ | $d\times d$ |

### 8.5 Connection to Kalman Filter

For

$$
x\sim\mathcal N(\mu,P)
$$

$$
y=Hx+\epsilon,\qquad
\epsilon\sim\mathcal N(0,R)
$$

the Kalman gain is

$$
K=PH^\top(HPH^\top+R)^{-1}
$$

The measurement update is

$$
\mu_{\mathrm{post}}
=
\mu+K(y-H\mu)
$$

$$
P_{\mathrm{post}}=P-KHP
$$

The Kalman measurement update is an application of
Gaussian conditioning.

---

## 9. Conditional Expectation and Bayesian Estimation

### 9.1 Conditional Expectation

$$
\boxed{
\mathbb E[x\mid y]
=
\int xp(x\mid y)\,dx
}
$$

### 9.2 MMSE Estimation

The minimum mean squared error estimator solves

$$
f^*
=
\arg\min_f
\mathbb E[\|x-f(y)\|^2]
$$

Its optimal solution is

$$
\boxed{
f^*(y)=\mathbb E[x\mid y]
}
$$

### 9.3 Derivation of MMSE

Define

$$
m=\mathbb E[x\mid y]
$$

Then

$$
x-\hat x=(x-m)+(m-\hat x)
$$

Taking conditional expectation:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathbb E[\|x-\hat x\|^2\mid y]
={}&\mathbb E[\|x-m\|^2\mid y]\\
&+\|m-\hat x\|^2\\
&+2\mathbb E[(x-m)^\top(m-\hat x)\mid y]
\end{aligned}
$$

Since

$$
\mathbb E[x-m\mid y]=0
$$

the cross term vanishes.

Therefore,

$$
\boxed{
\mathbb E[\|x-\hat x\|^2\mid y]
=
\mathbb E[\|x-m\|^2\mid y]
+
\|m-\hat x\|^2
}
$$

The minimum is attained at

$$
\hat x=m=\mathbb E[x\mid y]
$$

### 9.4 MAP Estimation

$$
\boxed{
\hat x_{\mathrm{MAP}}
=
\arg\max_x p(x\mid y)
}
$$

### Bayesian Estimation Comparison

| Method | Formula | Objective |
|---|---|---|
| MMSE | $\mathbb E[x\mid y]$ | Minimize expected squared error |
| MAP | $\arg\max_x p(x\mid y)$ | Maximize posterior density |
| Posterior sampling | $x\sim p(x\mid y)$ | Generate samples from the posterior |

For a nondegenerate Gaussian posterior:

$$
\hat x_{\mathrm{MMSE}}=\hat x_{\mathrm{MAP}}
$$

For general distributions, they may differ.

A posterior mean is not necessarily a high-density sample.

