## 12. Forward and Reverse Diffusion

### Forward Process

$$
x_0\rightarrow x_1\rightarrow\cdots\rightarrow x_T
$$

The forward process gradually attenuates the signal and
adds Gaussian noise.

### Reverse Process

$$
x_T\rightarrow x_{T-1}\rightarrow\cdots\rightarrow x_0
$$

The reverse process gradually generates data from noise.

### Important Probability Distributions

| Distribution | Meaning |
|---|---|
| $p(x_0)$ | Original data distribution |
| $q(x_t\mid x_{t-1})$ | Single-step forward transition |
| $q(x_t\mid x_0)$ | Cumulative forward transition |
| $q(x_t)$ | Forward marginal distribution |
| $q(x_{t-1}\mid x_t)$ | Exact reverse transition |
| $p_\theta(x_{t-1}\mid x_t)$ | Learned reverse transition |

The forward transition is predefined.

The exact reverse transition depends on the data distribution
and is generally intractable.

---

## 13. Single-Step Forward Diffusion

### Probability Distribution

$$
\boxed{
q(x_t\mid x_{t-1})
=
\mathcal N
\left(
x_t;
\sqrt{\alpha_t}x_{t-1},
(1-\alpha_t)I
\right)
}
$$

### Sampling Representation

$$
\boxed{
x_t=
\sqrt{\alpha_t}x_{t-1}
+
\sqrt{1-\alpha_t}\epsilon_t
}
$$

where

$$
\epsilon_t\sim\mathcal N(0,I)
$$

The noise is independent of the previous states.

### Noise Schedule

$$
\boxed{\alpha_t=1-\beta_t}
$$

$$
\boxed{
\bar\alpha_t=\prod_{s=1}^{t}\alpha_s
}
$$

$$
\bar\alpha_0=1
$$

### Parameter Definitions

| Parameter | Meaning |
|---|---|
| $\beta_t$ | Single-step noise variance coefficient |
| $\alpha_t$ | Single-step signal retention coefficient |
| $\sqrt{\alpha_t}$ | Signal scaling coefficient |
| $\sqrt{\beta_t}$ | Noise scaling coefficient |
| $\bar\alpha_t$ | Cumulative signal retention coefficient |
| $1-\bar\alpha_t$ | Cumulative noise variance coefficient |

The square roots are required because covariance scales
with the square of the coefficient.

---

## 14. Closed-Form Forward Diffusion

The cumulative forward transition is

$$
\boxed{
q(x_t\mid x_0)
=
\mathcal N
\left(
x_t;
\sqrt{\bar\alpha_t}x_0,
(1-\bar\alpha_t)I
\right)
}
$$

Equivalent sampling representation:

$$
\boxed{
x_t=
\sqrt{\bar\alpha_t}x_0
+
\sqrt{1-\bar\alpha_t}\epsilon
}
$$

where

$$
\epsilon\sim\mathcal N(0,I)
$$

### 14.1 Two-Step Derivation

$$
x_1=
\sqrt{\alpha_1}x_0+
\sqrt{1-\alpha_1}\epsilon_1
$$

$$
x_2=
\sqrt{\alpha_2}x_1+
\sqrt{1-\alpha_2}\epsilon_2
$$

Substitute $x_1$:

$$
\begin{aligned}
x_2
={}&\sqrt{\alpha_1\alpha_2}x_0\\
&+\sqrt{\alpha_2(1-\alpha_1)}\epsilon_1\\
&+\sqrt{1-\alpha_2}\epsilon_2
\end{aligned}
$$

The two independent noise terms have combined covariance

$$
\begin{aligned}
\Sigma_{\mathrm{noise}}
&=\alpha_2(1-\alpha_1)I+(1-\alpha_2)I\\
&=(1-\alpha_1\alpha_2)I
\end{aligned}
$$

Therefore,

$$
x_2=
\sqrt{\bar\alpha_2}x_0+
\sqrt{1-\bar\alpha_2}\epsilon
$$

where

$$
\bar\alpha_2=\alpha_1\alpha_2
$$

The same argument extends to arbitrary $t$ by induction.

### 14.2 Signal and Noise Evolution

| Stage | $\bar\alpha_t$ | Signal Scaling | Noise Scaling |
|---|---:|---:|---:|
| Beginning | 1 | 1 | 0 |
| Early | 0.9 | 0.949 | 0.316 |
| Intermediate | 0.5 | 0.707 | 0.707 |
| Late | 0.1 | 0.316 | 0.949 |
| Near pure noise | 0.01 | 0.1 | 0.995 |
| Limit | 0 | 0 | 1 |

If the schedule satisfies $\bar\alpha_t\to0$:

$$
x_t\rightarrow\mathcal N(0,I)
$$

in distribution.

### 14.3 Importance

Closed-form diffusion allows direct sampling of $x_t$
without performing all preceding diffusion steps.

This is essential for efficient DDPM training.

