## 15. Diffusion with a Gaussian Mixture Prior

### 15.1 Original Data Distribution

Problem 2 assumes

$$
\boxed{
p(x_0)
=
\sum_{k=1}^{K}
\pi_k\mathcal N(x_0;0,\Sigma_k)
}
$$

where

$$
\Sigma_k\succ0
$$

$$
\pi_k\geq0,\qquad
\sum_k\pi_k=1
$$

Introduce the latent component variable:

$$
p(c=k)=\pi_k
$$

$$
x_0\mid c=k\sim\mathcal N(0,\Sigma_k)
$$

All components have zero mean but may have different
covariance matrices.

### 15.2 Component Covariance after Diffusion

The target result is

$$
\boxed{
C_{k,t}
=
\bar\alpha_t\Sigma_k+
(1-\bar\alpha_t)I
}
$$

Therefore,

$$
\boxed{
x_t\mid c=k
\sim\mathcal N(0,C_{k,t})
}
$$

Relevant mathematical tools:

- Gaussian affine transformation.
- Addition of independent Gaussian variables.
- Covariance transformation.

### 15.3 Forward Marginal Distribution

After marginalizing the latent component variable:

$$
\boxed{
q(x_t)
=
\sum_{k=1}^{K}
\pi_k\mathcal N(x_t;0,C_{k,t})
}
$$

The forward marginal remains a Gaussian mixture.

The original mixture weights $\pi_k$ remain unchanged.
Only the component covariance matrices evolve.

### 15.4 Posterior Responsibility

The posterior component probability is

$$
\boxed{
w_{k,t}(x_t)
=
p(c=k\mid x_t)
=
\frac{
\pi_k\mathcal N(x_t;0,C_{k,t})
}{
\sum_{j=1}^{K}
\pi_j\mathcal N(x_t;0,C_{j,t})
}
}
$$

The prior weights remain fixed, but posterior weights depend
on both the observation and diffusion time.

### 15.5 Covariance Recursion

The required recursive form is

$$
\boxed{
C_{k,t}
=
\alpha_tC_{k,t-1}
+
(1-\alpha_t)I
}
$$

with initial condition

$$
C_{k,0}=\Sigma_k
$$

### Closed Form vs. Recursive Form

| Expression | Purpose |
|---|---|
| $C_{k,t}=\bar\alpha_t\Sigma_k+(1-\bar\alpha_t)I$ | Direct computation from initial covariance |
| $C_{k,t}=\alpha_tC_{k,t-1}+(1-\alpha_t)I$ | One-step covariance propagation |

### 15.6 Covariance Evolution

The covariance can also be written as

$$
C_{k,t}=I+\bar\alpha_t(\Sigma_k-I)
$$

Suppose

$$
\Sigma_k=U_k\Lambda_kU_k^\top
$$

Then

$$
C_{k,t}
=
U_k
[\bar\alpha_t\Lambda_k+(1-\bar\alpha_t)I]
U_k^\top
$$

Each eigenvalue evolves according to

$$
\lambda_{i,t}
=
1+\bar\alpha_t(\lambda_{i,0}-1)
$$

Consequently:

- Eigenvalues greater than 1 decrease toward 1.
- Eigenvalues smaller than 1 increase toward 1.
- Eigenvalues equal to 1 remain unchanged.
- The eigenvectors can be kept unchanged.

As $\bar\alpha_t\to0$:

$$
\boxed{
C_{k,t}\to I
}
$$

All mixture components converge to the same standard
Gaussian distribution.

Consequently, for a fixed observation,

$$
w_{k,t}(x_t)\to\pi_k
$$

The observation becomes uninformative about the original
component.

### Matrix Dimensions

| Variable | Dimension |
|---|---|
| $x_0,x_t,\epsilon$ | $d\times1$ |
| $\Sigma_k$ | $d\times d$ |
| $C_{k,t}$ | $d\times d$ |
| $I$ | $d\times d$ |
| $\alpha_t,\beta_t,\bar\alpha_t$ | Scalar |
| $\pi_k$ | Scalar |
| $w_{k,t}(x_t)$ | Scalar |
| $q(x_t)$ | Scalar |

### Problem 2(a) — Essential Results

$$
\boxed{
C_{k,t}=
\bar\alpha_t\Sigma_k+(1-\bar\alpha_t)I
}
$$

$$
\boxed{
q(x_t)=\sum_k\pi_k\mathcal N(x_t;0,C_{k,t})
}
$$

$$
\boxed{
w_{k,t}(x_t)=
\frac{\pi_k\mathcal N(x_t;0,C_{k,t})}
{\sum_j\pi_j\mathcal N(x_t;0,C_{j,t})}
}
