## 17. Forward vs. Reverse Transition

Forward transition:

$$
q(x_t\mid x_{t-1})
$$

Exact reverse transition:

$$
\boxed{
q(x_{t-1}\mid x_t)
=
\frac{
q(x_t\mid x_{t-1})q(x_{t-1})
}{
q(x_t)
}
}
$$

The forward transition is known, but the reverse transition
also requires the data marginals.

Therefore, reversing diffusion is a Bayesian inference
problem, not a deterministic algebraic inversion.

---

## 18. Joint Distribution of Consecutive States

Conditioned on component $k$:

$$
x_{t-1}\mid c=k
\sim\mathcal N(0,C_{k,t-1})
$$

$$
x_t\mid c=k
\sim\mathcal N(0,C_{k,t})
$$

The forward relationship is

$$
x_t=
\sqrt{\alpha_t}x_{t-1}
+
\sqrt{1-\alpha_t}\epsilon_t
$$

The relevant cross-covariance structure is

$$
\operatorname{Cov}(x,y)=\Sigma_xA^\top
$$

For the diffusion transition,

$$
A=\sqrt{\alpha_t}I
$$

Hence, the required joint covariance blocks are

| Block | Expression |
|---|---|
| $\Sigma_{xx}$ | $C_{k,t-1}$ |
| $\Sigma_{xy}$ | $\sqrt{\alpha_t}C_{k,t-1}$ |
| $\Sigma_{yx}$ | $\sqrt{\alpha_t}C_{k,t-1}$ |
| $\Sigma_{yy}$ | $C_{k,t}$ |

The target joint distribution is

$$
\boxed{
q(x_{t-1},x_t)
=
\sum_{k=1}^{K}
\pi_k\mathcal N
\left(
\begin{bmatrix}
x_{t-1}\\x_t
\end{bmatrix};
\begin{bmatrix}
0\\0
\end{bmatrix},
\begin{bmatrix}
C_{k,t-1}&\sqrt{\alpha_t}C_{k,t-1}\\
\sqrt{\alpha_t}C_{k,t-1}&C_{k,t}
\end{bmatrix}
\right)
}
$$

---

## 19. Component Reverse Transition

Each component is jointly Gaussian.

Therefore, its reverse transition is Gaussian:

$$
q(x_{t-1}\mid x_t,c=k)
=
\mathcal N(x_{t-1};m_{k,t},S_{k,t})
$$

### Conditional Mean

Apply the general Gaussian conditioning formula:

$$
\mu_{x\mid y}
=
\mu_x+
\Sigma_{xy}\Sigma_{yy}^{-1}(y-\mu_y)
$$

The target result is

$$
\boxed{
m_{k,t}
=
\sqrt{\alpha_t}
C_{k,t-1}C_{k,t}^{-1}x_t
}
$$

### Conditional Covariance

The unsimplified Gaussian conditioning expression is

$$
S_{k,t}
=
C_{k,t-1}
-
\alpha_t
C_{k,t-1}
C_{k,t}^{-1}
C_{k,t-1}
$$

The required simplified result is

$$
\boxed{
S_{k,t}
=
(1-\alpha_t)
C_{k,t-1}C_{k,t}^{-1}
}
$$

The relevant matrix identity is

$$
I-aAB^{-1}=bB^{-1}
$$

with

$$
A=C_{k,t-1}
$$

$$
B=C_{k,t}
$$

$$
a=\alpha_t,\qquad b=1-\alpha_t
$$

The detailed homework proof is reserved for Round 2.

