## 20. Exact Reverse Gaussian Mixture

Conditioning on $x_t$ changes the mixture weights from
prior probabilities to posterior responsibilities.

The required result is

$$
\boxed{
q(x_{t-1}\mid x_t)
=
\sum_{k=1}^{K}
w_{k,t}(x_t)
\mathcal N(x_{t-1};m_{k,t},S_{k,t})
}
$$

with

$$
m_{k,t}
=
\sqrt{\alpha_t}
C_{k,t-1}C_{k,t}^{-1}x_t
$$

$$
S_{k,t}
=
(1-\alpha_t)
C_{k,t-1}C_{k,t}^{-1}
$$

### Interpretation

| Quantity | Meaning |
|---|---|
| $w_{k,t}(x_t)$ | Probability of component $k$ given current sample |
| $m_{k,t}$ | Estimated previous state within component $k$ |
| $S_{k,t}$ | Remaining uncertainty within component $k$ |

### Conceptual Workflow

1. Fix the latent Gaussian component.
2. Construct the joint Gaussian distribution.
3. Apply Gaussian conditioning.
4. Combine the conditional Gaussians using posterior weights.

### Matrix Dimensions

| Variable | Dimension |
|---|---|
| $x_{t-1},x_t$ | $d\times1$ |
| Joint random vector | $2d\times1$ |
| $C_{k,t-1},C_{k,t}$ | $d\times d$ |
| $C_{k,t}^{-1}$ | $d\times d$ |
| Cross-covariance | $d\times d$ |
| Joint covariance | $2d\times2d$ |
| $m_{k,t}$ | $d\times1$ |
| $S_{k,t}$ | $d\times d$ |
| $w_{k,t}(x_t)$ | Scalar |

---

## 21. Exact Reverse Transition vs. DDPM Approximation

The exact reverse transition is generally not Gaussian.

For a Gaussian mixture prior, it is a Gaussian mixture.

Standard DDPM commonly parameterizes the reverse transition as

$$
p_\theta(x_{t-1}\mid x_t)
=
\mathcal N
\left(
x_{t-1};
\mu_\theta(x_t,t),
\Sigma_\theta(x_t,t)
\right)
$$

| Property | Exact Reverse | Learned DDPM Reverse |
|---|---|---|
| Notation | $q(x_{t-1}\mid x_t)$ | $p_\theta(x_{t-1}\mid x_t)$ |
| Distribution | Generally complex | Usually Gaussian |
| Parameters | Determined by data distribution | Model-predicted or predefined |
| Availability | Generally intractable | Computable after training |
| Role | True reverse process | Approximate reverse process |

For sufficiently small diffusion steps and suitable
regularity conditions, the reverse transition can be
approximated locally by a Gaussian.

