## 23. Score Function

### 23.1 Definition

$$
\boxed{
s(x)=\nabla_x\log p(x)
}
$$

Using the chain rule:

$$
\boxed{
s(x)=\frac{\nabla_xp(x)}{p(x)}
}
$$

Equivalently,

$$
\boxed{
\nabla_xp(x)=p(x)s(x)
}
$$

The score is a vector pointing in the direction of the
steepest local increase in log probability density.

It is not a probability or a probability density.

### Matrix Dimensions

| Variable | Dimension |
|---|---|
| $x$ | $d\times1$ |
| $p(x)$ | Scalar |
| $\log p(x)$ | Scalar |
| $\nabla_xp(x)$ | $d\times1$ |
| $s(x)$ | $d\times1$ |

### 23.2 Why Use the Log Density?

Suppose

$$
p(x)=\frac{f(x)}{Z}
$$

where

$$
Z=\int f(x)\,dx
$$

Then

$$
\begin{aligned}
\nabla_x\log p(x)
&=\nabla_x[\log f(x)-\log Z]\\
&=\nabla_x\log f(x)
\end{aligned}
$$

Thus,

$$
\boxed{
\nabla_x\log p(x)=\nabla_x\log f(x)
}
$$

The normalization constant disappears because it does not
depend on $x$.

Advantages:

- Eliminates the global normalization constant.
- Simplifies differentiation of exponential densities.
- Removes the density-dependent multiplicative scaling
  present in $\nabla_xp(x)$.

### 23.3 Score vs. Gradient Descent

| Property | Gradient Descent | Score-Based Gradient Ascent |
|---|---|---|
| Objective | $L(W)$ | $\log p(x)$ |
| Updated variable | Parameters $W$ | Sample $x$ |
| Direction | $-\nabla_W L$ | $+\nabla_x\log p(x)$ |
| Purpose | Decrease loss | Increase density locally |

A stationary score does not necessarily indicate a density
maximum. It may also indicate a minimum or saddle point.

---

## 24. Gaussian Score

Given

$$
p(x)=\mathcal N(x;\mu,\Sigma)
$$

take the logarithm:

$$
\log p(x)
=
-\frac d2\log(2\pi)
-\frac12\log|\Sigma|
-\frac12(x-\mu)^\top\Sigma^{-1}(x-\mu)
$$

The first two terms are constant with respect to $x$.

Therefore,

$$
\nabla_x\log p(x)
=
-\frac12
\nabla_x[(x-\mu)^\top\Sigma^{-1}(x-\mu)]
$$

Since $\Sigma^{-1}$ is symmetric,

$$
\nabla_x[(x-\mu)^\top\Sigma^{-1}(x-\mu)]
=
2\Sigma^{-1}(x-\mu)
$$

Hence,

$$
\boxed{
s(x)=-\Sigma^{-1}(x-\mu)
}
$$

### Special Cases

| Gaussian Distribution | Score |
|---|---|
| $\mathcal N(\mu,\sigma^2)$ | $-(x-\mu)/\sigma^2$ |
| $\mathcal N(0,1)$ | $-x$ |
| $\mathcal N(\mu,I)$ | $-(x-\mu)$ |
| $\mathcal N(0,\sigma^2I)$ | $-x/\sigma^2$ |
| $\mathcal N(\mu,\Sigma)$ | $-\Sigma^{-1}(x-\mu)$ |
| $\mathcal N(0,\Sigma)$ | $-\Sigma^{-1}x$ |

The precision matrix determines the direction and magnitude
of the Gaussian score.

For the same displacement, smaller variance along a principal
direction produces a larger score magnitude along that direction.

---

## 25. Gaussian Mixture Score

Given

$$
p(x)=\sum_k\pi_kp_k(x)
$$

define component scores:

$$
s_k(x)=\nabla_x\log p_k(x)
$$

The general mixture score identity is

$$
\boxed{
s(x)=\sum_k w_k(x)s_k(x)
}
$$

where

$$
w_k(x)=
\frac{\pi_kp_k(x)}
{\sum_j\pi_jp_j(x)}
$$

Relevant identities for the homework derivation:

$$
\nabla_x\log p(x)
=
\frac{\nabla_xp(x)}{p(x)}
$$

$$
\nabla_xp_k(x)=p_k(x)s_k(x)
$$

$$
\nabla_x\sum_k\pi_kp_k(x)
=
\sum_k\pi_k\nabla_xp_k(x)
$$

Important:

$$
s(x)\neq\sum_k\pi_ks_k(x)
$$

in general.

The mixture score uses posterior responsibilities,
not fixed prior mixture weights.

### Why Posterior Weights?

A component that is more likely to have generated the
current observation should contribute more strongly to
its score.

---

## 26. Score of the Diffused Gaussian Mixture

From Problem 2(a):

$$
q(x_t)=
\sum_k\pi_k\mathcal N(x_t;0,C_{k,t})
$$

Each component score is

$$
s_{k,t}(x_t)
=
-C_{k,t}^{-1}x_t
$$

The required result is

$$
\boxed{
s_t(x_t)
=
\nabla_{x_t}\log q(x_t)
=
-\sum_{k=1}^{K}
w_{k,t}(x_t)C_{k,t}^{-1}x_t
}
$$

### Effective Precision Matrix

Define

$$
\Lambda_{\mathrm{eff}}(x_t,t)
=
\sum_k w_{k,t}(x_t)C_{k,t}^{-1}
$$

Then

$$
s_t(x_t)
=
-\Lambda_{\mathrm{eff}}(x_t,t)x_t
$$

Each component score is linear in $x_t$.

However, the mixture score is generally nonlinear because
the posterior weights depend on $x_t$.

### Matrix Dimensions

| Variable | Dimension |
|---|---|
| $x_t$ | $d\times1$ |
| $C_{k,t}$ | $d\times d$ |
| $C_{k,t}^{-1}$ | $d\times d$ |
| $w_{k,t}(x_t)$ | Scalar |
| $s_{k,t}(x_t)$ | $d\times1$ |
| $\Lambda_{\mathrm{eff}}(x_t,t)$ | $d\times d$ |
| $s_t(x_t)$ | $d\times1$ |

---

## 27. Score Evolution during Diffusion

At $t=0$:

$$
C_{k,0}=\Sigma_k
$$

$$
s_0(x_0)
=
-\sum_k w_{k,0}(x_0)\Sigma_k^{-1}x_0
$$

As $\bar\alpha_t\to0$:

$$
C_{k,t}\to I
$$

$$
q(x_t)\to\mathcal N(0,I)
$$

For a fixed $x_t$,

$$
\begin{aligned}
s_t(x_t)
&\to-\sum_k w_{k,t}(x_t)x_t\\
&=-x_t
\end{aligned}
$$

Therefore,

$$
\boxed{s_t(x_t)\to-x_t}
$$

| Stage | Component Covariance | Score |
|---|---|---|
| $t=0$ | $\Sigma_k$ | Original data score |
| Intermediate $t$ | $C_{k,t}$ | Time-dependent mixture score |
| $\bar\alpha_t\to0$ | $I$ | Standard Gaussian score $-x_t$ |

A diffusion score network must account for the noise level:

$$
s_\theta(x_t,t)
$$

