## 29. Gaussian Observation Model

Consider

$$
\boxed{
y=Ax+b+\epsilon
}
$$

where

$$
\epsilon\sim\mathcal N(0,R)
$$

and $x$ and $\epsilon$ are independent.

Equivalently,

$$
q(y\mid x)=\mathcal N(y;Ax+b,R)
$$

The prior distribution $p(x)$ is not required to be Gaussian.

The marginal observation distribution is

$$
q(y)=\int q(y\mid x)p(x)\,dx
$$

### Matrix Dimensions

| Variable | Dimension |
|---|---|
| $x$ | $d\times1$ |
| $y$ | $m\times1$ |
| $A$ | $m\times d$ |
| $b$ | $m\times1$ |
| $\epsilon$ | $m\times1$ |
| $R$ | $m\times m$ |
| $\mathbb E[x\mid y]$ | $d\times1$ |
| $A\mathbb E[x\mid y]$ | $m\times1$ |
| $\nabla_y\log q(y)$ | $m\times1$ |

---

## 30. Tweedie's Formula

### 30.1 Generalized Formula

For

$$
q(y\mid x)=\mathcal N(y;Ax+b,R)
$$

with $R\succ0$:

$$
\boxed{
\nabla_y\log q(y)
=
-R^{-1}
\left(
y-A\mathbb E[x\mid y]-b
\right)
}
$$

Rearranging:

$$
\boxed{
A\mathbb E[x\mid y]
=
y-b+R\nabla_y\log q(y)
}
$$

If $A$ is invertible:

$$
\boxed{
\mathbb E[x\mid y]
=
A^{-1}
\left(
y-b+R\nabla_y\log q(y)
\right)
}
$$

If $A$ is not invertible, the projected posterior mean
$A\mathbb E[x\mid y]$ is determined, but the full posterior
mean may not be uniquely determined by this identity.

### 30.2 Classical Tweedie's Formula

For

$$
y=x+\epsilon,\qquad
\epsilon\sim\mathcal N(0,\sigma^2I)
$$

we have

$$
A=I,\qquad b=0,\qquad R=\sigma^2I
$$

Therefore,

$$
\boxed{
\mathbb E[x\mid y]
=
y+\sigma^2\nabla_y\log q(y)
}
$$

Using score notation:

$$
\boxed{
\mathbb E[x\mid y]=y+\sigma^2s(y)
}
$$

The posterior mean equals the noisy observation plus
a score-based correction.

| Term | Meaning |
|---|---|
| $y$ | Noisy observation |
| $s(y)$ | Marginal score |
| $\sigma^2$ | Noise variance |
| $\sigma^2s(y)$ | Denoising correction |
| $\mathbb E[x\mid y]$ | MMSE estimate |

### 30.3 Derivation of Tweedie's Formula

Start with marginalization:

$$
q(y)=\int q(y\mid x)p(x)\,dx
$$

Differentiate under the integral:

$$
\nabla_yq(y)
=
\int\nabla_yq(y\mid x)p(x)\,dx
$$

Use

$$
\nabla_yq(y\mid x)
=
q(y\mid x)\nabla_y\log q(y\mid x)
$$

Then

$$
\nabla_y\log q(y)
=
\int
\frac{q(y\mid x)p(x)}{q(y)}
\nabla_y\log q(y\mid x)\,dx
$$

Applying Bayes' rule:

$$
\boxed{
\nabla_y\log q(y)
=
\mathbb E[
\nabla_y\log q(y\mid x)
\mid y]
}
$$

This is the marginal score identity.

Since the observation is Gaussian,

$$
\nabla_y\log q(y\mid x)
=
-R^{-1}(y-Ax-b)
$$

Therefore,

$$
\begin{aligned}
\nabla_y\log q(y)
&=\mathbb E[-R^{-1}(y-Ax-b)\mid y]\\
&=-R^{-1}(y-A\mathbb E[x\mid y]-b)
\end{aligned}
$$

This establishes the generalized Tweedie's formula.

### 30.4 Tweedie's Formula vs. Gaussian Conditioning

| Property | Gaussian Conditioning | Tweedie's Formula |
|---|---|---|
| Prior requirement | Joint Gaussianity | General prior |
| Required information | Joint mean and covariance | Marginal score |
| Output | Conditional Gaussian distribution | Posterior mean or projected posterior mean |
| Application | Linear Gaussian inference | Score-based denoising |

---

## 31. Diffusion Posterior Mean

The diffusion observation model is

$$
x_t=
\sqrt{\bar\alpha_t}x_0
+
\sqrt{1-\bar\alpha_t}\epsilon
$$

where

$$
\epsilon\sim\mathcal N(0,I)
$$

### Correspondence with Gaussian Observation Model

| General Model | Diffusion Model |
|---|---|
| $x$ | $x_0$ |
| $y$ | $x_t$ |
| $A$ | $\sqrt{\bar\alpha_t}I$ |
| $b$ | $0$ |
| $R$ | $(1-\bar\alpha_t)I$ |
| $s(y)$ | $s_t(x_t)$ |
| $\mathbb E[x\mid y]$ | $\mathbb E[x_0\mid x_t]$ |

Using Tweedie's formula, the required result is

$$
\boxed{
\mathbb E[x_0\mid x_t]
=
\frac{
x_t+(1-\bar\alpha_t)s_t(x_t)
}{
\sqrt{\bar\alpha_t}
}
}
$$

where

$$
s_t(x_t)=\nabla_{x_t}\log q(x_t)
$$

The factor $1/\sqrt{\bar\alpha_t}$ compensates for
the attenuation of the original signal.

