## 32. Linear Denoiser

Suppose

$$
x\sim\mathcal N(0,\Sigma_x)
$$

$$
y=Ax+\epsilon
$$

$$
\epsilon\sim\mathcal N(0,R)
$$

Gaussian conditioning gives

$$
\mathbb E[x\mid y]
=
\Sigma_{xy}\Sigma_{yy}^{-1}y
$$

Using

$$
\Sigma_{xy}=\Sigma_xA^\top
$$

$$
\Sigma_{yy}=A\Sigma_xA^\top+R
$$

we obtain

$$
\boxed{
\mathbb E[x\mid y]
=
\Sigma_xA^\top
(A\Sigma_xA^\top+R)^{-1}y
}
$$

Define

$$
G=
\Sigma_xA^\top
(A\Sigma_xA^\top+R)^{-1}
$$

Then

$$
\boxed{\hat x=Gy}
$$

This is a linear MMSE denoiser.

For a nonzero Gaussian prior mean, the corresponding
estimator is generally affine rather than purely linear.

### Scalar Gaussian Denoiser

For

$$
x\sim\mathcal N(0,\sigma_x^2)
$$

$$
y=x+\epsilon
$$

$$
\epsilon\sim\mathcal N(0,\sigma_\epsilon^2)
$$

we have

$$
\boxed{
\mathbb E[x\mid y]
=
\frac{\sigma_x^2}
{\sigma_x^2+\sigma_\epsilon^2}y
}
$$

The denoising gain is

$$
g=\frac{\sigma_x^2}
{\sigma_x^2+\sigma_\epsilon^2}
$$

| Noise Variance | Denoising Behavior |
|---|---|
| $\sigma_\epsilon^2\to0$ | Estimate approaches observation |
| Moderate noise | Estimate combines prior and observation |
| $\sigma_\epsilon^2\to\infty$ | Estimate approaches prior mean |

This behavior is called shrinkage.

---

## 33. Mixture of Linear Denoisers

For a Gaussian mixture prior:

$$
p(x)=\sum_k\pi_k\mathcal N(x;0,\Sigma_k)
$$

each component has a linear posterior mean:

$$
\mathbb E[x\mid y,c=k]=G_ky
$$

where

$$
G_k=
\Sigma_kA^\top
(A\Sigma_kA^\top+R)^{-1}
$$

By the law of total expectation:

$$
\boxed{
\mathbb E[x\mid y]
=
\sum_k w_k(y)G_ky
}
$$

Each individual denoiser is linear.

However, the overall mixture denoiser is generally nonlinear
because its weights depend on the observation.

Define

$$
G_{\mathrm{eff}}(y)=\sum_k w_k(y)G_k
$$

Then

$$
\mathbb E[x\mid y]=G_{\mathrm{eff}}(y)y
$$

The effective matrix is input-dependent.

