# Part III — Connection to DDPM

## 36. Conditional Score vs. Marginal Score

### Conditional Score

From

$$
q(x_t\mid x_0)
=
\mathcal N
\left(
x_t;
\sqrt{\bar\alpha_t}x_0,
(1-\bar\alpha_t)I
\right)
$$

the Gaussian score is

$$
\nabla_{x_t}\log q(x_t\mid x_0)
=
-\frac{x_t-\sqrt{\bar\alpha_t}x_0}
{1-\bar\alpha_t}
$$

Using

$$
x_t-\sqrt{\bar\alpha_t}x_0
=
\sqrt{1-\bar\alpha_t}\epsilon
$$

we obtain

$$
\boxed{
\nabla_{x_t}\log q(x_t\mid x_0)
=
-\frac{\epsilon}{\sqrt{1-\bar\alpha_t}}
}
$$

### Marginal Score

Using the marginal score identity:

$$
\nabla_{x_t}\log q(x_t)
=
\mathbb E[
\nabla_{x_t}\log q(x_t\mid x_0)
\mid x_t]
$$

Therefore,

$$
\boxed{
s_t(x_t)
=
-\frac{
\mathbb E[\epsilon\mid x_t]
}{
\sqrt{1-\bar\alpha_t}
}
}
$$

The conditional score depends on the original clean sample.

The marginal score does not require access to the original
clean sample.

---

## 37. Noise Prediction Network

DDPM trains a neural network

$$
\epsilon_\theta(x_t,t)
$$

using the noise prediction objective:

$$
\boxed{
L(\theta)
=
\mathbb E_{x_0,t,\epsilon}
\left[
\|\epsilon-\epsilon_\theta(x_t,t)\|^2
\right]
}
$$

By the MMSE property, the theoretical optimal predictor is

$$
\epsilon^*(x_t,t)
=
\mathbb E[\epsilon\mid x_t,t]
$$

Hence, the predicted marginal score is

$$
\boxed{
s_\theta(x_t,t)
=
-\frac{\epsilon_\theta(x_t,t)}
{\sqrt{1-\bar\alpha_t}}
}
$$

Noise prediction provides an indirect method for learning
the marginal score.

---

## 38. Clean Sample Prediction

Using the score-based posterior mean:

$$
\hat x_0
=
\frac{
x_t+(1-\bar\alpha_t)s_\theta(x_t,t)
}{
\sqrt{\bar\alpha_t}
}
$$

Substitute

$$
s_\theta(x_t,t)
=
-\frac{\epsilon_\theta(x_t,t)}
{\sqrt{1-\bar\alpha_t}}
$$

to obtain

$$
\boxed{
\hat x_0
=
\frac{
x_t-\sqrt{1-\bar\alpha_t}\epsilon_\theta(x_t,t)
}{
\sqrt{\bar\alpha_t}
}
}
$$

If the noise predictor is optimal under MSE, this clean
sample prediction equals the posterior mean.

### Prediction Target Conversion Table

| Conversion | Formula |
|---|---|
| Noise to score | $s_\theta=-\epsilon_\theta/\sqrt{1-\bar\alpha_t}$ |
| Score to noise | $\epsilon_\theta=-\sqrt{1-\bar\alpha_t}s_\theta$ |
| Noise to clean sample | $\hat x_0=(x_t-\sqrt{1-\bar\alpha_t}\epsilon_\theta)/\sqrt{\bar\alpha_t}$ |
| Score to clean sample | $\hat x_0=(x_t+(1-\bar\alpha_t)s_\theta)/\sqrt{\bar\alpha_t}$ |
| Clean sample to noise | $\epsilon_\theta=(x_t-\sqrt{\bar\alpha_t}\hat x_0)/\sqrt{1-\bar\alpha_t}$ |

These parameterizations can be converted into one another
for a fixed nondegenerate diffusion timestep.

Different training losses and weighting schemes may still
produce different training behavior.

