## 40. Posterior Mean vs. Reverse Sampling

| Property | Reverse Transition | Clean Sample Estimation |
|---|---|---|
| Expression | $q(x_{t-1}\mid x_t)$ | $\mathbb E[x_0\mid x_t]$ |
| Output | Probability distribution | Vector |
| Target | Previous diffusion state | Original clean sample |
| Uncertainty | Explicitly represented | Averaged |
| Purpose | Generative sampling | Denoising estimation |

A posterior mean does not necessarily represent a realistic
sample from a multimodal distribution.

If $x_T$ becomes independent of $x_0$, then

$$
\mathbb E[x_0\mid x_T]=\mathbb E[x_0]
$$

Therefore, directly predicting the posterior mean from
pure noise cannot generally produce diverse samples.

Diffusion models instead use an iterative reverse process:

$$
x_T\rightarrow x_{T-1}\rightarrow\cdots\rightarrow x_0
$$

