## 43. Common Mistakes

- Confusing $\alpha_t$ and $\bar\alpha_t$.
- Confusing variance with standard deviation.
- Forgetting to square scaling coefficients when computing
  covariance.
- Assuming diffusion always increases total variance.
- Assuming Gaussian mixture distributions are themselves
  Gaussian.
- Assuming Gaussian marginals imply a joint Gaussian.
- Assuming zero covariance always implies independence.
- Interchanging matrix multiplication order without
  justification.
- Confusing cross-covariance with individual covariance.
- Forgetting the inverse in Gaussian conditioning.
- Using prior mixture weights instead of posterior
  responsibilities.
- Treating the score as a scalar probability.
- Differentiating the score with respect to model parameters
  instead of the sample.
- Assuming the mixture score is linear because its
  component scores are linear.
- Assuming Tweedie's formula requires a Gaussian prior.
- Assuming MMSE and MAP are always identical.
- Assuming the posterior mean is a posterior sample.
- Confusing the true reverse transition with the learned
  Gaussian reverse transition.
- Confusing the clean-sample posterior mean with the
  previous-state posterior mean.
- Assuming deterministic gradient ascent performs sampling.

