## 44. High-Priority Quiz Concepts

1. Gaussian affine transformations preserve Gaussianity.

2. Gaussian diffusion modifies both the signal and noise,
   driving covariance toward the identity matrix.

3. The forward marginal of a Gaussian mixture is a Gaussian
   mixture with unchanged prior weights.

4. The reverse transition requires posterior mixture weights.

5. Gaussian conditioning provides both the conditional mean
   and conditional covariance.

6. The exact reverse transition is not generally a single
   Gaussian distribution.

7. The score is the gradient of the log probability density
   with respect to the sample.

8. A Gaussian score is affine, while a zero-mean Gaussian
   score is linear.

9. A mixture score is a posterior-weighted combination of
   component scores.

10. Tweedie's formula connects the marginal score to the
    posterior mean of the clean data.

11. The posterior mean is the optimal MSE estimator.

12. A mixture of linear denoisers is generally nonlinear.

13. Noise prediction, score prediction and clean-sample
    prediction are convertible representations.

14. Posterior mean estimation and generative sampling
    are fundamentally different operations.